read the Miyoo Mini's battery off its ADC

The Mini has no `/sys/class/power_supply` and no `axp_test`: the first is
upstream's source and the second is the Mini Plus's, so slot showed
`no battery` on a handheld whose charge is perfectly readable. It is an ADC
reading rather than a file -- the SAR controller behind `/dev/sar`, two
vendor ioctls on it, and a GPIO that says whether the charger is in.

Found by `strings` on Onion's own `batmon`, which named `/dev/sar` and
gpio59; implemented from Allium's
`crates/common/src/platform/miyoo/battery/miyoo283.rs`, which runs on this
handheld. Neither the ioctl numbers nor the curve are guessable, and reading
the two projects that already do it is cheaper and more honest than fitting a
curve to a battery nobody here can measure.

  - `_IO('a', 0)` initialises the controller, `_IO('a', 1)` reads a channel
    into `{ channel, value }`.
  - The percentage is four straight segments of that value, Allium's: 578 and
    up is full, 528..577 is `v - 478`, 512..527 and 480..511 each have their
    own slope, and below that is empty.
  - gpio59 reads non-zero while charging, exported first if the kernel has
    not already.

The curve is kept exactly as Allium has it, including a 2-point step down at
the 527/528 join -- 51.9% then 50%. Smoothing it would be inventing a better
fit for a cell that cannot be measured here, which is worse than inheriting a
wart. A test pins the segment edges and allows that step while catching
anything larger.

Third in the chain, after the vendor tool and before sysfs: a handheld with
`axp_test` has no `/dev/sar`, and one with sysfs is not a Miyoo.

Read on the handheld: the gauge shows a percentage where it showed nothing.

Applies to upstream 91499a9.

